     "result_text_tail": "dropped; one robustness claim went further than the source; and the next steps sat after the appendix.\n\n**Corrections to the source (all logged)**\n- Gross-margin levers come to **$17.0M**, not $16.9M. The source added rounded values.\n- The high case is **$37.9M** of levers and **$45.6M** of operating income, not $38.0M and $45.7M. The margin is still about 3.2%.\n- The shrink sensitivity is **\u2212$2.2M**, not \u2212$2.1M.\n- The source said the plan is \"most sensitive to traffic and vendor funding\". Its own table shows a 6% wage step (\u2212$3.6M) mattering more than traffic (\u2212$2.0M). That slide now says these are \"the key risks\", which matches the source's executive summary.\n\n**Numbers the pack can't reproduce**\nI kept these as the analysis's own estimates and marked them as not rebuilt from the pack on their slides:\n- Option C's $42.9M (closing home delivery)\n- $3.0M from second-wave delivery pricing\n- The store-exit low and high cases ($3.0M and $5.0M)\n- The $1.0M private-label gain in FY2026\n- The 2.4% mid-FY2027 checkpoint threshold\n\n**Choices I made on my own**\n- Navy theme.\n- American spelling.\n- The fiscal-timing caveat from the source is kept: if FY2026 has closed by the board date, its covenant result becomes a fact to report.\n\nNo \"?\" placeholders are left. Next, I could switch the theme or tighten the three densest tables (the options comparison, the lever table and the sensitivity table).\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- harlow-margin-plan.pptx\n- harlow-margin-plan-sources.xlsx"
    }
